{"description":"The transport invariants were one bespoke test per field, so the only oracle for the exchange format was the implementation itself: 'the specification' meant whatever eka-core did that day, and a fixture-driven case could not be added without new Go. That is the missing half of the spec-anchored posture the project chose — prose cannot be wrong when nothing executes it, and prose alone did not catch any of the four wave-5 envelope defects.","acceptanceCriteria":"- `exchange/testdata/corpus/` holds one fixture repository per case plus `cases.json`; a new case is a fixture directory and one manifest entry, no Go change.\n- Each entry states the dotted paths the exported unit must carry, the paths the written artifact must carry, and (for the envelope-free case) the paths it must NOT carry.\n- The round trip is asserted byte-identical: export, import into an empty repository, re-export, compare packages.\n- A manifest sanity test fails when a fixture directory is unlisted or an entry points at nothing, so a dropped fixture cannot silently stop being tested.\n- The corpus is published in the standard repository under `conformance/exchange/` with `SHA256SUMS` and a README stating the contract, and the implementation's copy is verified against those bytes by `scripts/verify-release.sh`.\n- Fixtures carry no absolute paths or host state and move verbatim between repositories.","debtRationale":""}